From f058f00c9fcf10786241e00ba1a2fd3891fb1e7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Karel Zak <kzak@redhat.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 11:13:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 94/94] libmount: pin the mount target for non-root users
 [CVE-2026-53613]

The target of a non-root mount always comes from fstab, but any
component of that path may be a symlink the user controls.
mnt_context_prepare_target() resolved it with mnt_resolve_path(), which
follows symlinks with root privileges, and do_mount() then handed the
result to mount(2), which resolved the string for a second time:

	fstab:  /dev/sdb1 /mnt/usb vfat user,noauto 0 0
	user:   rmdir /mnt/usb; ln -s /etc /mnt/usb
	result: mount lands on /etc

For restricted contexts the target is now opened as it comes from fstab
with ul_open_no_symlinks() (openat2(RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS)), so a symlink
anywhere in the path is refused by the kernel, and the name is read back
from /proc/self/fd/<fd>.  The name is symlink free and normalized, and
it is the very name the kernel later reports in mountinfo, which is what
utab and non-root umount need.  This also means that a symlink in the
target path of an fstab entry is no longer usable by non-root users,
root has to write the real path.

mount(2) takes no file descriptor, so the pinned FD is passed to it as
the /proc/self/fd/<fd> magic link, which the kernel follows directly to
the pinned directory; there is a fallback to the plain path when /proc
is not available.  The source of a bind or move operation is a pathname
resolved by mount(2) too and is pinned in the same way.

Some mounts need more than one mount(2) call: a bind mount which also
sets security flags, and any mount which sets a propagation flag.  The
additional calls of do_mount_additional() run when the filesystem is
already attached and they address the target by pathname again, so the
pin does not protect them; and because the kernel ignores the security
flags on a bind operation, the filesystem is usable without nosuid,
nodev and noexec until the second call applies them.  For restricted
users such a multi-step sequence is now refused in fix_optstr(), before
any syscall.

Backport notes:

 * The patches are taken from the upstream stable/v2.42 branch; each of
   the commits referenced below records the corresponding master commit
   in its own message.  This is the rhel/10 patch (8d0aa1036) reduced to
   what 2.37 needs.

 * Hooksets are 2.39+.  This branch has no hook_mount.c,
   hook_mount_legacy.c or hook_owner.c, so this is not a cherry-pick but
   the same change written into the old context_mount.c do_mount() flow.

 * There is no new mount API here, mount(2) is the only path.  The
   fd_tree / open_tree() / MOVE_MOUNT_T_EMPTY_PATH hunks of 0d3d55975
   and 472cc3fb2 are dropped; only the mount(2) half of 85d16b341 is
   taken.

 * CVE-2026-53612 does not apply to this branch and its commits
   (d0c5adaeb, bc43adb27, 70dae206e and the hook_owner / hook_idmap
   hunks of 472cc3fb2) are not backported.  2.37 has neither
   X-mount.owner=, X-mount.group=, X-mount.mode= nor X-mount.idmap=;
   X-mount.mkdir is the only X-mount option and it already returns
   -EPERM for restricted contexts.

 * mnt_context_finalize_target() and the mount ID handling
   (mnt_fs_fetch_ids(), mnt_id_from_fd(), mnt_id_from_path()) are not
   backported either.  Nothing re-uses the target FD once the
   filesystem is attached -- there are no MNT_STAGE_POST hooks -- and
   2.37 has no statx() probe and no ID in the utab entry.

 * The refusal of the multi-step mount(2) sequence is the legacy half of
   31e37c1c7; upstream has the two mnt_context_is_restricted() checks in
   hook_prepare() of hook_mount_legacy.c, here they are in fix_optstr(),
   which is the place where this branch decides about the additional
   mount(2) calls.  The other half of that commit -- safe_getenv() for
   LIBMOUNT_FORCE_MOUNT2 [CVE-2026-53614] -- is not applicable, 2.37 has
   no new mount API and no such variable.

 * Upstream can still do these mounts because the new mount API applies
   the flags in one step; 2.37 has mount(2) only, so a non-root "bind"
   or propagation entry in fstab stops working.  libmount returns -EPERM
   before any syscall, mount(8) then drops the privileges and retries
   (the "Try it again without permissions" path in main()), and the
   kernel refuses the mount.  Upstream ends up with the same message
   whenever the new mount API is not available.

 * The pin failure is reported by DBG() only; this branch has no
   mnt_context_sprintf_mesg() and the message would not be printed by
   mnt_context_get_mount_excode() on the preparation path anyway.  The
   user sees the kernel error -- "mount failed: Too many levels of
   symbolic links" for a symlinked target.

Verified with a SUID mount.static and "strace -u nobody", fstab entry
"/tmp/ultest/src /tmp/ultest/mnt tmpfs noauto,user 0 0":

	openat2(AT_FDCWD, "/tmp/ultest/mnt", {flags=O_RDONLY|O_CLOEXEC|O_PATH,
					      resolve=RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS}, 24) = 3
	mount("/tmp/ultest/src", "/proc/self/fd/3", "tmpfs",
	      MS_NOSUID|MS_NODEV|MS_NOEXEC, NULL) = 0

and with /tmp/ultest/mnt replaced by a symlink:

	openat2(AT_FDCWD, "/tmp/ultest/mnt", {...}, 24) = -1 ELOOP
	mount.static: /tmp/ultest/mnt: mount failed: Too many levels of symbolic links.

With "/tmp/ultest/src /tmp/ultest/mnt none bind,noauto,user 0 0" the two
mount(2) calls of the old code

	mount("/proc/self/fd/4", "/proc/self/fd/3", 0x26dee2e0,
	      MS_NOSUID|MS_NODEV|MS_NOEXEC|MS_BIND, NULL) = 0
	mount("none", "/tmp/ultest/mnt", NULL,
	      MS_NOSUID|MS_NODEV|MS_NOEXEC|MS_REMOUNT|MS_BIND, NULL) = 0

(the third argument is printed as a pointer because the kernel ignores
the filesystem type for these operations) are replaced by

	setgid(99)  = 0
	setuid(99)  = 0
	setuid(0)   = -1 EPERM (Operation not permitted)
	mount("/tmp/ultest/src", "/tmp/ultest/mnt", 0x186de2f0,
	      MS_NOSUID|MS_NODEV|MS_NOEXEC|MS_BIND, NULL) = -1 EPERM
	mount.static: /tmp/ultest/mnt: must be superuser to use mount.

where the syscall is the unprivileged retry of mount(8) and nothing is
mounted.  Root still gets both calls.

CVE-2026-53613

